    "source_file_to_entries": {
        "raw-wiktextract-data.jsonl": [
            {
                "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                "word": "syrinx",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "2890c0ba-271c-41d5-99a8-1945eb5c09db",
                        "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "syrinxes"
                    },
                    {
                        "form_id": "dd673b73-5c6c-4964-9e86-0e35d70079ca",
                        "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "syringes"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                        "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A set of panpipes."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "d0d73b46-633f-42f9-b0b3-d2d408c60973",
                                "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                                "text": "[T]he Syrinx might give occaſion to the bagpipe, by leading the vvay to its invention; for it vvas certainly very natural, both for eaſe in playing, and for the ſaving of breath, and even for the health and ſafety of the performer's lungs, to contrive a method of conveying vvind to the ſeveral pipes by means of bellovvs."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "38e7c692-cb9f-4921-87c4-11f0a724d82b",
                                "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                                "text": "Pipes will I fashion of the syrinx flag, / That thou mayst always know whither I roam, / When it shall please thee in our quiet home / To listen and think of love."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "088bc755-1b3f-42ab-b987-4d8f5c911226",
                                "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                                "text": "In Dr. Sven Hedin's collection there is a fragment of a neck of a jar, which shows a whole circle of Gandharvas performing on drums, harps, syrinxes, etc. Noteworthy is the existence of the syrinx on artware of Eastern Turkestan. That instrument has never, so far as I am aware, been observed in Indian art."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8d8194fb-4410-4eda-b5ab-0951e691fba5",
                                "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                                "text": "The Panpipe is indeed one of the most characteristic instruments of South America, especially of the Western regions. the ancient Peruvian civilizations produced syrinxes not only of reeds but also of terra cotta (those of the coastal civilizations, especially of Nazca, are sometimes real works of art) and even carved in stone, chiefly among the Araucans."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7613f6dc-ad06-4775-8c3b-a0673c59395a",
                                "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                                "text": "Actually, to cut a long story short, he began … well, playing with a rather different sort of pipe. Or syrinx, as we called it. He obviously thought he was alone. I was frankly quite shocked. Disgusted. It wasn't that I hadn't seen naked men before, at my aunt's in Cyprus."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "46eaae52-e019-4bd8-8145-32989689d4df",
                                "sense_id": "4785bb91-8199-34e0-bb86-5a14f5ac8b9c",
                                "text": "Inside, somebody was playing a duet on syrinx and lyre. Lew thought he knew the tune, but then it went off in some direction he couldn't follow."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "c0c31bd7-3c3b-3343-9504-8bad6117105a",
                        "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Chiefly in the plural: a narrow channel cut in rock, especially in Ancient Egyptian burial chambers."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "52408137-68bf-441f-8a32-ad87999c8597",
                                "sense_id": "c0c31bd7-3c3b-3343-9504-8bad6117105a",
                                "text": "And novv vvhen the people of all ſorts came flocking to the Court, in a tumultuous manner, Agathocles [grandson of Agathocles of Syracuse], taking the King vvith him, vvent and hid himſelf, in a place called Syringes, vvhich vvas a gallery or vvalk, vvhich had every vvay three vvalls and gates to paſſe, before one could come unto it."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7f98e794-a8d9-4d88-ac29-8be0225cdc3c",
                                "sense_id": "c0c31bd7-3c3b-3343-9504-8bad6117105a",
                                "text": "The Former of theſe Tvvo Hermes [i.e., the god Thoth], vvas the Inventor of Arts and Sciences, the Latter [Siphoas], the Reſtorer and Advancer of them: the Firſt vvrote in Hieroglyphicks upon Pillars, ἐν τῆ Συριγγικῆ γῆ [in the land of Syringes] (as the learned Valeſius conjectures it ſhould be read, instead of Σηριαδικῆ.) VVhich Syringes vvhat they vvere, Am[mianus] Marcellinus vvill inſtruct us; […]"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "304ce128-fd25-4909-b249-d1cd09f2f4fe",
                                "sense_id": "c0c31bd7-3c3b-3343-9504-8bad6117105a",
                                "text": "There are alſo (in Egypt) Syrinxes, certain ſubterraneous and vvinding receſſes, vvhich (as it is ſaid) men skilled in antient rites, foreknovving the coming of the deluge, and fearing that the memory of their ceremonies ſhould be blotted out, hevved and faſhioned out of quarries in ſeveral places, vvith immenſe labour, and carved on the vvalls vvhich they had ſo hevvn, many kinds of fovvls, and vvild beaſts, and figures of animals innumerable, vvhich they called hieroglyphick (ſo ſome read the text) or ſacred characters."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "f92d54aa-543b-4725-ba58-fddc32fe0a0a",
                                "sense_id": "c0c31bd7-3c3b-3343-9504-8bad6117105a",
                                "text": "Further the Ramesseion (the Osmandyeion of the Descript.) with the alley of sphinxes, the Menephtheion (palace at Kurnah) and other monuments as late as Ptolemy the First's time. Grottoes and syrinxes all around."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "1353c856-1fda-333d-9c33-d4d9f8c5f6b9",
                        "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Any of several abnormal tube-shaped structures in the body, especially a rare, fluid-filled neuroglial cavity in the brain stem or within the spinal cord."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "218ad617-4cb6-49c2-8ce3-d809b71d1b4c",
                                "sense_id": "1353c856-1fda-333d-9c33-d4d9f8c5f6b9",
                                "text": "The division of fluid-filled cavities, or syrinxes, into those lined with ependyma (hydromyelia) and those not so lined (syringomyelia) is probably somewhat artificial."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "7761050d-8435-4fed-9a2c-336261f79e13",
                                "sense_id": "1353c856-1fda-333d-9c33-d4d9f8c5f6b9",
                                "text": "The myelin around the syrinx shows changes similar to edematous white matter, probably due to tearing of the tissues and transudation of serous fluid into them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "81ce8836-2b8d-4327-92e8-0ec5f79d0d44",
                                "sense_id": "1353c856-1fda-333d-9c33-d4d9f8c5f6b9",
                                "text": "A syrinx (Latin, \"tube\") is a neuroglial cell-lined, fluid-filled cavity (\"syringomyelia\" in the spinal cord; \"syringobulbia\" in the brain stem) possibly due to an accumulation of cerebrospinal fluid, genetic malformations, or the proliferation and subsequent regression of embryonic cell nests. A syrinx within the central canal interrupts the decussating spinothalamic tract fibers resulting in bilateral loss of pain and temperature sensations around the level of the lesion."
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
                        "sense_id": "de83e735-ce9e-3b4f-bd15-2030b5d8bc9c",
                        "entry_id": "1dfe8898-3d22-3800-ae1b-2e29545e0202",
                        "glosses": [
                            "The voice organ in birds, located at or near where the trachea and the bronchi join."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "broadly"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "acc17ea2-a901-4e6b-b1bd-7a7617bacc0d",
                                "sense_id": "de83e735-ce9e-3b4f-bd15-2030b5d8bc9c",
                                "text": "This tube is named the Windpipe, Trachea, or Aspera-arteria; […] its lower extremity, or the part at which it bifurcates, is the Lower Larynx, or the Syrinx. Besides acting as a pipe to the lungs, it is also the organ of the voice, the air in passing through it causing its membranes so to vibrate, and being so acted upon by the muscles attached to it, as to give rise to the various cries and notes emitted by birds, although the palate, the tongue, and the mandibles also operate in modifying the sounds thus produced."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "12603cc5-a705-4b07-ad95-ad1c43a6d19e",
                                "sense_id": "de83e735-ce9e-3b4f-bd15-2030b5d8bc9c",
                                "text": "The role of the syrinx in psittacine sound production is still under examination, and probably differs from that of songbirds. […] For species with relatively simple syringes but complex vocal behavior (such as Grey parrots), these data imply that other structures must modify syringeal output."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "3a1ecbe7-9da9-4db3-9ed4-4852fc2735b8",
                                "sense_id": "de83e735-ce9e-3b4f-bd15-2030b5d8bc9c",
                                "text": "The primary sound-producing organ in a bird is the syrinx and the secondary system aiding sound production consists of the larynx, mouth, tongue and laryngeal muscles. […] Birds vocalise by expelling air over the elastic membranes of the syrinx housed within the inter-clavicular sac, an air sac in the pleural cavity."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "182a494c-e867-4da8-957d-35d0c630e7d8",
                                "sense_id": "de83e735-ce9e-3b4f-bd15-2030b5d8bc9c",
                                "text": "The organ responsible for producing sounds during song is the syrinx, located where the trachea joins the bronchi of the two lungs[…]. Four to six muscles on either side are attached to the syrinx, and sound is produced when air is expelled through it. The flow of air induces part of the syrinx wall to vibrate and generate sound, in a similar way to the operation of the human larynx."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
